ha256" "83f37d13971d1d37ec7e30988c3313f61caf3d4e510265e73b44fceaee237569" "basis" 1974 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The support of $X$ is $S_X = \\{0,1\\},$ the support of $Y$ is $S_Y = \\{0,1\\},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS= S_X\\times S_Y = \\left\\{(0,0),  (0,1),  (1,0),  (1,1) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{a}{2}+a+a+\\frac{5}{2}a & =1 \\\\ 5a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/example-9126.json" "file_sha256" "83f37d13971d1d37ec7e30988c3313f61caf3d4e510265e73b44fceaee237569" "basis" 1974 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P\\Big( (X,Y) \\in \\{(2,3), (6,1) \\}\\Big)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/example-9203.json" "file_sha256" "0cc334ec9a524c7aebcf66ffae1dcd26f8a7e27f0fe442090c28d01d54621dfa" "basis" 1974 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in {(2,3),(6,1)}) & =P((X,Y)=(2,3))+P((X,Y)=(6,1)) \\\\  & =f(2,3)+f(6,1) \\\\  & =0.25+0.3 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/example-9203.json" "file_sha256" "0cc334ec9a524c7aebcf66ffae1dcd26f8a7e27f0fe442090c28d01d54621dfa" "basis" 1974 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left( X\\cdot Y \\geq 4 \\right)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/example-9204.json" "file_sha256" "734d99b667a52eb3a0b390e635d94858e8497995c13439e4a7c38110154dbe21" "basis" 1974 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\cdot Y\\ge 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(1,4),(2,2),(2,4)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(1,4))+P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(1,4)+f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.25+0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/example-9204.json" "file_sha256" "734d99b667a52eb3a0b390e635d94858e8497995c13439e4a7c38110154dbe21" "basis" 1974 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-110812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $a$ | $2a$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{a}{2}$ | $5a$ |\n| $X=3$ | $3a$ | $\\frac{3a}{2}$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The support of $X$ is ${S}_{X}={1,2,3},$ the support of $Y$ is ${S}_{Y}={0,1},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}={(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1),(3,0),(3,1)}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0)+f(1,1)+f(2,0)+f(2,1)+f(3,0)+f(3,1) & =1 \\\\ a+2a+\\frac{a}{2}+5a+3a+\\frac{3a}{2} & =1 \\\\ 13a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a95abc9dbf1014f0fd42a2ebbc90e95e65ca663e6f022132972e58f996c7892"} {"question" "q-110812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{11}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{17}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{13}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.33$ | $0.07$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions is true?\n\n- $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$\n- ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$\n- $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition I is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition II is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.42+0.18+0.33+0.07=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions I and II, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a95abc9dbf1014f0fd42a2ebbc90e95e65ca663e6f022132972e58f996c7892"} {"question" "q-143866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $a$ | $2a$ | $a$ |\n| $X=1$ | $3a$ | $a$ | $2a$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The support of $X$ is ${S}_{X}={0,1},$ the support of $Y$ is ${S}_{Y}={1,2,3},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}={(0,1),(0,2),(0,3),(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1)+f(0,2)+f(0,3)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3) & =1 \\\\ a+2a+a+3a+a+2a & =1 \\\\ 10a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a95abc9dbf1014f0fd42a2ebbc90e95e65ca663e6f022132972e58f996c7892"} {"question" "q-110810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-110810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions is true?\n\n- $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$\n- ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$\n- $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d95"} {"question" "q-143865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition I is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition II is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.4+0.3+0.2+0.1=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions I and II, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072641/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17b471c17cbc8d8b65a72e2e3eb3651d24fbc446c9937df7263b0c30d4d1d9